urban proximity enables social  interactions some good and some bad when  those interactions are voluntary like  sharing a meal or getting a manicure  then the odds are that everyone expects  to benefit otherwise they'd stay home  but some involuntary interactions like a  mugging or a murder  can do great harm to the whole web of  urban life cities enable crime by making  it easier for thieves or bullies to have  physical access to their victims cities  can make it harder to catch criminals by  increasing the number of suspects in a  classic British murder mystery set in  perhaps a country estate  the clever detective need only sort out  the motives and whereabouts of a handful  of potential perpetrators if Hercule  Poirot at trying to solve a mystery in a  teeming London slum then the number of  suspect will be vast and finding the  culprit might exceed the limits of even  his numerous little gray cells for  millennia cities have paid for law  enforcement professionals to keep city  streets safe indeed the connection  between police and cities is so deep  that the very word police has its roots  in the Greek word polis which means city  law itself is not a particularly urban  thing the English common law evolved for  centuries focusing on the problems of  protecting property rights for rural  land but paying for professional police  only makes sense when they are  protecting enough people police are  essentially a fixed cost that needs to  be shared and sharing that cost requires  sufficient scale preventive patrolling  only makes sense when enough people are  being protected to justify the costs of  the patrolman's time ancient Athens had  something of a police force 300 City and  slaves specialized archers kept order  this was a pretty small number relative  to Athens population which may have  exceeded 300,000 by 430 BC but the  Citians were used more to prevent riots  than to prosecute criminals for normal  crimes Athenians had to press their own  cases in front of 200 or more jurors  Augustus brought order to Roman chaos  with two distinct forms of police three  years after a fire damaged his own home  the princeps created a force of about  7,000 freed slaves in six ad to watch  over the Roman Knight they carried  buckets to fight fires but they also  started fighting crime as well  Augustus also relied on the Cortes  Urbana and his Praetorian Guard  who  more military force to stop riots and  serious public disorders when Rome fell  Europe returned to agrarian ways and  organized public policing largely  disappeared there were fraternal  organizations like the holy  Brotherhood's in Spain or the vemma  courts in Germany that came together to  serve as vigilantes towns might also  have night watchmen  armed primarily with a rattle to raise  the human cry in the event of a  disturbance The Watchmen might serve  under a constable but he was a local  official not a patrolling policeman  wealthy private individuals such as  merchants or powerful clerics might also  hire their own armed guards France is  typically credited with being the  birthplace of modern policing King  Francis the first instituted a roving  military police meant chiefly to check  the plundering of his own soldiers king  louis xiv centralized the french police  force in 1666 and his police would be  particularly focused on paris by the  18th century paris would have over 1,000  policemen and one police chief boasted  to Louis the 15th that whenever three  people speak to one another on the  street one of these will be mine  suggesting the repressive political role  that police would also play during the  Bourbon monarchy the English feared that  oppressive power and so it wasn't until  1829 that Parliament passed the  Metropolitan Police Act to provide a  modern police force for London English  police have been known as both bobbies  and peelers after Sir Robert Peel who  introduced the Act  fears of police abuse have long kept  English police lightly armed carrying  only clubs rather than the revolvers and  automatic weapons that became the norm  both on the continent and in the more  dangerous United States America followed  the English lead in 1838 Boston  instituted its police force which was  organized into the Boston Police  Department in 1854 New York City  organized its own Police Department in  1845 just as in many developing world  cities today America's 19th century  police forces were consistently accused  of corruption laws refloated and  payments would be made to police  officers to turn a blind eye some of the  payments to individual policemen would  work their way up the system and end up  in reaching the coffers of political  machines such as Tammany Hall reformers  would consistently try to fix the system  and some reforms did actually occur  especially when state legislators were  politically opposed to locally  in 1857 New York's overwhelmingly  Republican state legislature decided to  disband New York City's corrupt  municipal police force that served its  Democratic mayor Fernanda wood and to  replace it with a Metropolitan Police  Force that would be led by a former Whig  congressman would initially refused to  disband his municipal police even after  the state Supreme Court ruled against  him the Metropolitan Police then  attempted to take over City Hall but the  Municipal fought back and what became  known as the great police riot the  Metropolitan's were defeated and they  retreated to the office of the city  recorder to tend their wounds eventually  the 7th regiment stepped in and would  surrendered temporarily but the rivalry  between the two forces continued and New  York suffered a crime spree culminating  in the deadly gang war known as the dead  rabbits riot after another adverse Court  ruling would eventually disbanded his  municipal police force but the  corruption of New York City's police  department continued during the 19th  century heyday of Tammany Hall in 1894  Republican state legislators again  turned their gaze upon the  democratically controlled police force  and an inquiry the Lex out committee  exposed the enormous irregularities  alexander williams known as clubber  because of his strong arm tactics was a  particular target of the investigation  when Williams was appointed to the  city's West 13th Street Station in 1876  he relished the opportunities for  extracting bribes from the abundant  saloons and brothels nearby he famously  said I've been having chuck steak ever  since I've been on the force and now I'm  going to have a bit of tenderloin  henceforth the neighborhood will be  known as the Tenderloin district when  the Lexile Commission wondered at how  Williams could afford his cost cop  estate and his Yacht Club or explained  that he had earned his great wealth by  speculating in Tokio real estate the  lexo committee's revelations led to the  election of a reforming mayor William  strong who appointed the young Teddy  Roosevelt to be a police commissioner  Roosevelt started appointing officers  based on merit not political patronage  and he prowled the streets at night to  see if this policemen were awake and  doing their jobs  he's a sigil sin forcement of the city's  anti-alcohol blue laws actually help  strong lose his reelection bid there  were just some laws that New Yorkers  didn't want to have enforced the  Progressive Era enacted numerous police  reforms officers were increasingly  appointed based on exams  professionalization and police rotation  tried to  the ties between policemen and local  businesses that enabled corruption by  the 1960s citizens became increasingly  intolerant to police brutality and  America's cops were criticized for doing  too much and too little as crime rates  surged during the 1970s many observers  thought that social policy is not police  with a solution but crime couldn't be  stopped with public largesse alone and  so under Rudy Giuliani New York again  embraced a strong police force the cops  began to target high crime areas and  followed a strategy known as broken  windows policing that targeted small  quality of life crimes the city did  become much safer and New York now as a  police force that is both effective and  generally quite honest the pleasures of  the city can now be enjoyed without fear  both day and night  the downside of that success is that too  many have been locked up thousands were  imprisoned for minor offenses an  ordinary New Yorkers chafed under  strong-arm tactics like stop and frisk  the task of making city streets safe is  only half-finished  crimes a fraction of what it was but we  have not yet figured out how to fight  crime without imprisoning thousands of  young men  you
 

